      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Developer Elzey Eugene Meachem sold 982 lots for less than $100 each in 1890, creating one of America’s first African‑American neighborhoods.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_Mound%2C_Memphis",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The original subdivision featured 982 shotgun houses, a distinctive style that reflected the era’s affordable housing strategy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://tennesseeencyclopedia.net/entries/orange-mound/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Melrose High School and Dunbar Elementary sit in Orange Mound, serving as focal points of community pride.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_Mound%2C_Memphis",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Ford‑funded Orange Mound Collaborative runs an Early Childhood Institute and an oral‑history project that records elders’ memories.",
